WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.486
API serving introduces concurrency.

00:00:01.486 --> 00:00:04.258
Instead of one user waiting on one response,

00:00:04.258 --> 00:00:08.415
the system may receive many requests with different prompt lengths, generation limits,

00:00:08.415 --> 00:00:09.108
and urgency.

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:13.613
Batch tuning becomes a scheduling problem as much as a raw performance problem.

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:17.077
The service has to decide how many requests to accept,

00:00:17.077 --> 00:00:20.889
how much work to group together, and when to apply backpressure.

00:00:20.889 --> 00:00:24.007
Continuous batching and dynamic batching are common serving ideas.

00:00:24.007 --> 00:00:25.393
At a high level,

00:00:25.393 --> 00:00:29.551
they let the runtime group active requests in ways that improve hardware

00:00:29.551 --> 00:00:32.669
utilization while requests arrive and finish at different times.

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:36.480
This can improve throughput, but it does not remove latency tradeoffs.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.945
Users still care about queue delay, first-token latency, and timeouts.

00:00:39.945 --> 00:00:41.677
Concurrency limits, maximum prompt size,

00:00:41.677 --> 00:00:45.835
and maximum generated tokens are part of batch tuning because they shape

00:00:45.835 --> 00:00:47.567
the work entering the system.

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:52.418
Without limits, a few very large prompts can consume memory and delay everyone else.

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:53.111
Fairness matters.

00:00:53.111 --> 00:00:57.269
A service should avoid letting one request pattern dominate the queue or

00:00:57.269 --> 00:00:59.348
make normal interactive requests feel unreliable.

00:00:59.348 --> 00:01:01.080
Operational controls complete the picture.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.852
Rate limiting protects the service from demand spikes.

00:01:03.852 --> 00:01:08.356
Backpressure tells clients to slow down instead of silently building an unhealthy queue.

00:01:08.356 --> 00:01:12.860
Monitoring should show queue depth, active sequences, token rates, errors, and resource use.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.632
The best throughput configuration is not just fast;

00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:18.404
it is understandable and recoverable when demand changes.

00:01:18.404 --> 00:01:21.522
For API platforms, define the service promise before tuning.

00:01:21.522 --> 00:01:24.987
Decide whether the priority is low latency for interactive users,

00:01:24.987 --> 00:01:28.798
high throughput for batch jobs, or a controlled balance between both.

00:01:28.798 --> 00:01:32.956
Those goals lead to different queue limits, timeout values, and batching choices.

00:01:32.956 --> 00:01:37.460
Without that service promise, a faster benchmark can still produce a worse product.
